    "content": "The new commander of the United States Africa Command (AFRICOM), General Dagvin Anderson, has clarified that the US does not intend to build a military installation in Somaliland. His remarks come after a series of visits by American officials to Hargeisa and the port city of Berbera, which had sparked speculation about a possible new US military footprint in the region.\n\nDuring his statement, General Anderson emphasized that Washington's stance on the Horn of Africa remains unchanged. He reiterated that the United States continues to back the independence, unity, and territorial integrity of the Federal Republic of Somalia. The clarification serves to quell rumors and alleviate concerns that the US might be shifting its long-standing diplomatic position.\n\nThe speculation had grown in recent weeks following high-level meetings between US officials and Somaliland leaders. Berbera, a strategic port on the Gulf of Aden, has attracted international interest due to its location near major shipping lanes. However, General Anderson's remarks make it clear that a military base is not part of US plans, despite the port's potential strategic value.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this news provides reassurance that US policy remains consistent. Many within the diaspora have deep ties to both Somalia and Somaliland, and any shift in US posture could have significant implications for regional stability. The explicit reaffirmation of support for Somalia's federal sovereignty is likely to be welcomed by those who feared a possible change in Washington's approach.\n\nThe statement also underscores the delicate balance of alliances in the region. While Somaliland has sought greater international recognition, the US continues to defer to the federal government in Mogadishu on matters of territorial integrity. This development serves as a reminder of the complex geopolitical dynamics at play, even as the region faces ongoing security challenges.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/01/mareykanka-somaliland-saldhig-ciidan-kama-dhisaneyno/",
